================================================================================
    SECTION 1: FOUNDATIONS OF OPTIONS SWING TRADING
================================================================================

Options swing trading combines the leverage of derivatives with the patience of
multi-day holds. Unlike day trading options, swing trades capture moves that
develop over 3-15 trading days, allowing you to use wider stops and ride
meaningful price swings.

KEY CONCEPTS FOR OPTIONS SWING TRADERS:

INTRINSIC VALUE: The real, tangible value of an option. A call with a strike of
$100 on a stock trading at $110 has $10 of intrinsic value.

EXTRINSIC VALUE: The time premium and implied volatility component. This is
what decays over time (theta) and is what makes option buying a depreciating
asset. Swing traders must account for this decay in every trade.

DELTA: Measures how much the option price moves for every $1 move in the
underlying stock. For swing trades, target options with a delta between 0.50
and 0.70. This gives you meaningful exposure without overpaying for premium.

THE GREEKS THAT MATTER MOST FOR SWINGS:
    Delta: Directional exposure (aim for 0.50-0.70)
    Theta: Time decay cost per day (the enemy of option buyers)
    Vega: Sensitivity to implied volatility changes
    Gamma: Rate of delta change (accelerates profits on big moves)

================================================================================
    SECTION 2: THE SIZE FOR ZERO METHOD EXPLAINED
================================================================================

The Size for Zero method is a risk management technique that transforms options
trading from gambling into a structured business. The core principle: always
plan to recover your initial investment quickly, then let the remaining
position ride risk-free.

HOW IT WORKS:

Step 1 - POSITION ENTRY: Buy your full position in one lot. For example,
purchase 10 contracts of AAPL $180 calls at $5.00 each. Total cost: $5,000.

Step 2 - FIRST TARGET: When the option appreciates 50-100%, sell HALF your
position. If those calls reach $10.00, sell 5 contracts for $5,000. You have
now recovered your entire initial investment.

Step 3 - FREE RIDE: The remaining 5 contracts have a cost basis of zero. You
cannot lose money on this trade regardless of what happens next. Let these
contracts ride with a trailing stop or toward a larger technical target.

Step 4 - FINAL EXIT: Close the remaining position at your technical target or
when momentum fades. Any profit from here is pure upside with zero risk.

WHY THIS WORKS: Most traders lose because they hold full positions through
adverse moves, hoping for a recovery. Size for Zero removes hope from the
equation. You either hit your first target and ride free, or you cut the
full position at your predefined stop loss.

POSITION SIZING RULE: Never risk more than 3-5% of your trading account on any
single options trade. If your account is $50,000, your maximum position size is
$1,500-$2,500 in premium.

================================================================================
    SECTION 3: STRIKE SELECTION AND EXPIRATION TIMING
================================================================================

Choosing the right strike price and expiration date is where most options
traders fail. Here are the rules for swing trading:

EXPIRATION TIMING: Always buy options with at least 30-45 days until
expiration, even if you plan to hold for only 5-10 days. This buffer protects
you from accelerating theta decay that hits options hard inside 21 days to
expiration (DTE).

STRIKE SELECTION FRAMEWORK:
    - Slightly In-The-Money (ITM): Delta 0.55-0.65. Best for high-conviction
      directional trades. Less extrinsic value to decay.
    - At-The-Money (ATM): Delta ~0.50. Balanced risk/reward with moderate
      theta exposure.
    - AVOID deep Out-of-The-Money (OTM): Delta below 0.30. These are cheap
      for a reason. They require large moves to profit and decay rapidly.

VOLATILITY AWARENESS: Check the Implied Volatility Rank (IVR) before buying.
If IVR is above 50, options are expensive relative to their historical range.
Consider selling premium or waiting for a volatility contraction. Buy options
when IVR is below 30 for the best value.

EARNINGS AVOIDANCE: Never hold swing trade options through earnings
announcements unless that is your specific strategy. The implied volatility
crush after earnings can destroy 30-50% of an option's value overnight, even
if the stock moves in your direction.

================================================================================
    SECTION 4: MANAGING WINNERS AND LOSERS
================================================================================

WINNER MANAGEMENT (Size for Zero in action):
    - At 50% gain: Sell half, move stop to breakeven on remainder
    - At 100% gain: Sell another quarter if you still hold more than 2 contracts
    - Let final portion ride with a trailing stop 20-25% below peak value

LOSER MANAGEMENT:
    - Maximum loss per trade: The full premium paid (never add to losers)
    - Cut at 40-50% loss if the technical thesis is invalidated
    - Time stop: Exit if the trade has not moved in your favor within 5 days
    - Never average down on a losing options position

TRADE JOURNAL METRICS TO TRACK:
    1. Win rate (target 45-55% for swing options)
    2. Average winner vs. average loser ratio (target 2:1 or better)
    3. Percentage of trades that reach Size for Zero target
    4. Average holding period for winners vs. losers
    5. Theta cost per trade (how much time decay ate into profits)

THE WEEKLY REVIEW PROCESS:
Every weekend, review your closed trades from the week. Categorize each trade
as either: followed the plan (win or lose), or deviated from the plan. The goal
is 100% plan adherence. Profitable deviations are just as dangerous as
unprofitable ones because they reinforce bad habits.

Options swing trading with the Size for Zero method gives you asymmetric
risk-reward on every trade. Your losses are small and defined. Your winners
can run indefinitely with no risk. Over hundreds of trades, this edge
compounds into consistent profitability.

